/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  The reactions of the ammonia screen.  Every equation is balanced; the
  element count of each side is written beside it (C, H, O, N).

  THE TWO PRODUCTION ROUTES, as overall input-output equations.  The
  synthesis step, N2 + 3 H2 -> 2 NH3, is the SAME in every route; what the
  routes differ in is where the hydrogen and the nitrogen come from, and
  that is all an overall equation keeps.

    electrolyticRoute   3 H2O + N2 -> 2 NH3 + 3/2 O2
                        water electrolysis (H2), air separation (N2),
                        synthesis.                    H6 O3 N2 = H6 O3 N2

    reformingRoute      0.884157 CH4 + 1.231686 H2O + 0.268314 O2 + N2
                          -> 2 NH3 + 0.884157 CO2
                        steam reforming of methane, the water-gas shift,
                        AIR in a secondary reformer, synthesis.  The air
                        brings the nitrogen; its oxygen is burnt inside the
                        process, so the oxygen is TIED to the nitrogen by
                        air's own composition: O2/N2 = 0.2095/0.7808 =
                        0.2683145... (data/standards/mixtures/air.dat),
                        taken as 0.268314 so that every coefficient has six
                        exact decimals.  The other two then FOLLOW from the
                        element balance -- they are not data:
                          O:  y + 2a = 2x     H:  4x + 2y = 6
                          =>  x = 3/4 + a/2 = 0.884157 (CH4)
                              y = 2x - 2a   = 1.231686 (H2O)
                        C0.884157 H6 O1.768314 N2 = C0.884157 H6 O1.768314 N2
                        This is the IDEAL plant: every carbon atom leaves as
                        CO2 and none is burnt for heat.  The fuel a real
                        reformer fires is not in it (see the README).
                        Grey and blue ammonia share this equation; blue
                        captures the CO2 instead of emitting it, which no
                        reaction can show.

  THE THREE NUMBERS A SCREEN NEEDS BESIDE THEM.

    waterSplitting      H2O -> H2 + 1/2 O2            H2 O1 = H2 O1
                        the electrolyser alone: its reaction enthalpy at
                        298.15 K is the least energy a thermoneutral
                        electrolyser can be supplied with per mole of H2.

    methaneCombustion   CH4 + 2 O2 -> CO2 + 2 H2O     C1 H4 O4 = C1 H4 O4
                        the fuel: its heat of reaction on the ideal-gas rung
                        IS the lower heating value of methane, which turns a
                        declared fuel energy into kilograms of methane and of
                        CO2.

    ammoniaSynthesis    N2 + 3 H2 <=> 2 NH3           H6 N2 = H6 N2
                        the step every route shares; its equilibrium is why
                        the next rung needs a recycle.

  No kinetics: a screen tries not to need a rate law yet.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

electrolyticRoute
{
    limitingReactant   N2;
    stoichiometry
    (
        { component water;  nu -3;   }
        { component N2;     nu -1;   }
        { component NH3;    nu  2;   }
        { component O2;     nu  1.5; }
    );
}

reformingRoute
{
    limitingReactant   N2;
    stoichiometry
    (
        { component CH4;    nu -0.884157; }
        { component water;  nu -1.231686; }
        { component O2;     nu -0.268314; }
        { component N2;     nu -1;        }
        { component NH3;    nu  2;        }
        { component CO2;    nu  0.884157; }
    );
}

waterSplitting
{
    limitingReactant   water;
    stoichiometry
    (
        { component water;  nu -1;   }
        { component H2;     nu  1;   }
        { component O2;     nu  0.5; }
    );
}

methaneCombustion
{
    limitingReactant   CH4;
    stoichiometry
    (
        { component CH4;    nu -1; }
        { component O2;     nu -2; }
        { component CO2;    nu  1; }
        { component water;  nu  2; }
    );
}

ammoniaSynthesis
{
    limitingReactant   N2;
    stoichiometry
    (
        { component N2;     nu -1; }
        { component H2;     nu -3; }
        { component NH3;    nu  2; }
    );
}
